					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/lady-parnassus" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1258005); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1258005&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1609167691" alt="Lady Parnassus" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1258005)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/lady-parnassus" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1258005); return false;" >Lady Parnassus</a></span> <span class="odate time_1323836083 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">14 Dec 2011 04:14</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-1327840">
									

<p>1. You could easily get rid of the incident report and condense it into some line like &quot;After Incident Blah and the loss of 8 researchers, further research has been suspended.&quot; As it is, the incident report is too heavily redacted and too&#8230; impersonal to get into, and I think it's dragging the main article down.<br />
2. The picture: At no point in the article is there mention of a replica being made, so the picture gave me pause and pulled me out of the article (That and &quot;it must not be photographed&quot;, which made me question the presence of the picture at all). I can see 3 ways of fixing it: Get rid of the picture, say it's a photo of a different (non-lethal) copy, or put in a picture of a cover.<br />
3. The line &quot;additional testing would be necessary for a statistically significant sample size&quot;. Not that anything about it bothers me, but I just thought of something that would be kind of neat. You need data on the book, you have to throw a D-class in there at regular intervals. Exempt the D-Class from termination, throw them in solitary with a camera. Over time, you would accumulate quite a bit of data. Just a thought.<br />
4. Footnote 2 and the sentence it's attached to also took me out of the article for a moment. A fourth-century counterpart to the Foundation?<br />
5. I agree with FuzzyBoots below. Curiosity and the fact that it's an apparently harmless book are temptation enough.</p>
<p>Aside from 1, these are all minor quibbles and I like the idea of it. Witholding my vote pending changes.</p>
<p>Edit: I derped on grammar for a minute.</p>

