														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-1332446">
									

<p>You misunderstand. I meant for no <em>good</em> or <em>interesting</em> reason, which after reading your entire post, I'd amend to 'no good or interesting reason that is actually present in the article'.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>As to the effects being obvious</p>
</blockquote>
<p>By &quot;obvious&quot; I didn't mean &quot;easy to see&quot;, I meant &quot;an obvious choice on the part of the writer, and therefore a boring choice&quot;.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Since very few people in the 4th century were literate, making the manuscript affect people who merely looked at it would allow it to help more people than if it were simply limited to those who could actually read written words.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>Okay now THAT is interesting.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>I also am trying to indicate that there may be a &quot;cure&quot; for the effects, possibly by overcoming the temptations, but that for whatever reason the Foundation research teams haven't figured it out yet.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>&#8230;Well why the hell not?!</p>
<p>4th century anomalous morality tool sounds really interesting. I understand wanting to stick by 'less is more', but in this case all the interesting elements are in the 'more'. Maybe it's just me, but I think you should put those things into the article, and assuming I liked the execution I'd happily upvote.</p>
<p>To be clear, if the effects were interesting in and of themselves, I wouldn't be recommending that. But they aren't.</p>

							</div>

								
					
